The UCI equivalent is ‘position ...‘ followed by ‘evalbar‘.                  UCI-Protocol-and-Stockfish-Commands.html
The engine emits a score line and a machine-readable link
line containing ‘hash‘, ‘occ‘, ‘material‘, ‘elo‘, confidence,
‘suspect‘, and ‘reason‘. The interface is documented in
‘docs/evalbar-elo-bitboard-link.md‘.

   The connection is deliberately one-way and read-only. It
cannot change the Elo model, move selection, search limits,
NNUE accumulator, or random state. This separation is
important: a display feature must not become a hidden
playing-strength feature.

               IV. Testing and Performance

   The complete core library passed 144 tests with zero
failures and six ignored tests. The eval-bar-specific suite
passed eight tests. These tests cover bitboard-key equality,
side-to-move normalization, rule-50 damping, WDL sum
conservation, fast-versus-exact WDL agreement, symmetry,
monotonicity, bounded smoothing, and the incremental-
state seam.

   The performance benchmark uses the repository’s seven-
position legal Matetrack EPD corpus. It runs 10,000 rounds
after tensor warm-up. The exact method evaluates the
public logistic equations with ‘exp‘; the fast method loads
the precomputed tensor.

   The final UCI smoke test returned ‘cp 14‘, ‘wdl 32 957 11‘,
‘bar 0.510‘, and ‘source=hce-proxy‘ for the start position.
Its link line returned position hash ‘1780828403df6413‘,

